95
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:07,320
We haven't seen each other or you guys haven't seen each other as a group in five months,

96
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:09,320
so it's good to have you back.

97
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:12,320
We have five things on the calendar for business tonight.

98
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:18,320
Historical and funding overview you'll see in the financial review under new business this evening.

99
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:22,320
The calendar and goals are also under new business.

100
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:24,320
And that fifth component is the legislative update.

101
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:28,320
I'll address through my report to you as the superintendent of the schools.

102
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:31,320
First, our opening day report.

103
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:38,320
All of our schools reported kids getting into the building at a 98 or 99% enrollment clip on day one.

104
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:41,320
So as it's reported to the school board, I just wanted to thank those families.

105
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:46,320
It's a tough transition to kind of end that summer and abruptly get back into the school system.

106
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:47,320
But day one is important.

107
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:48,320
It sets the tone.

108
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:52,320
So we were very happy to have more than 3,000 kids with us on that opening day.

109
00:10:52,320 --> 00:11:00,320
I want to give you an update on some school board recent determinations and discussions that they've had as a group over the past several months.

110
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:03,320
First is they have voted to change the time of their meetings.

111
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:07,320
They're going to start beginning their meetings at 6 30 p.m.

112
00:11:07,320 --> 00:11:10,320
And that will take effect at the next board meeting here in September.

113
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:17,320
They've also made a decision in a determination to change the deliberative session date for 2027.

114
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:24,320
If you recall, we've engaged with you before and kept you up to speed here in this committee with the ongoings of school board conversations with the board of selectmen.

115
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:26,320
I've had some reach out to the community.

116
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:31,320
They've reviewed some survey results, but the board made that determination to change that day.

117
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:35,320
They've identified that date is Saturday, February 6th.

118
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:40,320
It is the second Saturday in the calendar out wide or stipulated in the RSA.

119
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:45,320
The school board is going to continue to talk and deliberate around time to designate for that time.

120
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:47,320
The survey times were in the a.m.

121
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:48,320
They've had those conversations.

122
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:49,320
They haven't set that time yet.

123
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:59,320
And they're also discussing location and whether the pack is an appropriate place to continue to be able to account for all the people that are demonstrating participation that day.

124
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:02,320
So we'll keep you up to speed on those decisions as well.

125
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:06,320
Their last meeting, they also voted to retain some funds.

126
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:10,320
I believe that decision was about $500,000 from the unassigned fund balance.

127
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:14,320
And they'll have that to draw on for any certain emergencies that may arise this year.

128
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:21,320
Many of you recall if the board determines to spend that money, they will hold by a statute of public hearing.

129
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:26,320
And they also can only expend that money through a full vote of a board at a publicly convened meeting.

130
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:30,320
But they did make that detention to retain some funds.

131
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:34,320
At the most recent meeting, they also solidified their board goals.

132
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:37,320
So I'm going to share those with you after this meeting electronically.

133
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:42,320
I think it's good that you have a copy of those so you can see what they're striving to achieve this year.

134
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:48,320
And in addition to that, their most recent meeting, they had asked me to present a fiscal year 26 summary.

135
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:50,320
So there's a lot of narrative that I did around that.

136
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:55,320
So I'm going to tune you into the board meeting, but I'm also going to share that whole executive summary with you.

137
00:12:55,320 --> 00:13:02,320
It does a pretty good job of starting in March of 2025 and walking through aligning the proposed to the voted budget.

138
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:14,320
And then some of the decisions the school district and board made over the course of the year to be able to get to the end of the year with a budget or with expenditures that fall within the parameters of those budgets.

139
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:17,320
So that's what the school boards been talking about and doing over the past months.

140
00:13:17,320 --> 00:13:24,320
As an update for this group, we expect health insurance rates to be released to us into New Hampshire residents on November.

141
00:13:24,320 --> 00:13:33,320
I'm sorry, October 7th. So it's not great timing, but we will be back in front of you that next day on October 8th to present the first discussions around personnel.

142
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:37,320
So we'll have that rate for us the day before we get to sit with you.

143
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:40,320
So I think that's going to be somewhat of good timing for us.

144
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:45,320
Two things to keep in mind with the fiscal 28 budget calendar that we're going to be working through.

145
00:13:45,320 --> 00:13:52,320
The school board also is going to be negotiating the TTA contract as the largest organization here in Timberland.

146
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:54,320
That is our contract with our teachers.

147
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:58,320
So that is going to be potentially a warrant in front of voters in March as well.

148
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:04,320
And the second contract they may be negotiating is the TAU contract and remind you that those are our building administrators,

149
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:10,320
our principals, our assistant principals, our directors, those administrators working primarily directly with children.

150
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:18,320
That could be also be a potential contract that's for residents in March as well to keep in mind as we're shaping that prospective budget.

151
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:27,320
In terms of summer 26, I want to give you an update on some work around the facilities department on a big, huge global scale.

152
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:34,320
We installed 62,000 feet of code compliant insulated roofing across the district this year.

153
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:37,320
In the summertime, most of that was done at Pollard.

154
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:42,320
In terms of fresh air systems, the district installed 11 new energy recovery units.

155
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:45,320
And again, a predominant number of those were over at Pollard.

156
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:50,320
Pollard School also increased their energy efficiency upgrade by 50 percent.

157
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:55,320
And the district fixed 17 broken or leaking skylights across the district.

158
00:14:55,320 --> 00:14:57,320
So a lot of summer work there.

159
00:14:57,320 --> 00:15:03,320
What's coming up on the TAP for CIP facilities this year is that they have targeted the high school bathrooms,

160
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:07,320
high school envelope cabinets from Pollard,

161
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:12,320
and also the Danville roof as projects that are going to be attacking here in fiscal 27.

162
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:15,320
In terms of the legislative update, and I'll end there, Sue,

163
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:18,320
there's three key things that I want to bring to your attention.

164
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:24,320
The first is something that we've talked here before about House Bill 1300.

165
00:15:24,320 --> 00:15:33,320
It primarily had two pieces that set a district cap on SAU spending and then a district tax cap on overall spending.

166
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:35,320
That was newly enacted.

167
00:15:35,320 --> 00:15:44,320
I wanted to bring an update to you that on August 31st, there was a court motion filed in Merrimack Superior Court that's challenging that law.

168
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:53,320
It really is seeking an emergency court injunction to prevent that question from showing up on the ballot on the November 26 election.

169
00:15:53,320 --> 00:15:56,320
And it fundamentally states three things of violation of the Constitution.

170
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:59,320
One, the language itself is confusing.

171
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:02,320
Two, it has misleading claims in the legislation.

172
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:06,320
And three, it's got improper bundling of options for voters.

173
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:08,320
So we're going to track that as a school district.

174
00:16:08,320 --> 00:16:13,320
It has consequences and implications for these discussions with this group.

175
00:16:13,320 --> 00:16:18,320
So any updates along those lines, we'll be bringing back to this group.

176
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:25,320
Second piece of legislation I want to bring your attention to is around the default budget.

177
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:30,320
So for a long history, the default budget sat with the hands of the school boards,

178
00:16:30,320 --> 00:16:35,320
and they were statutorily responsible and obligated for putting it forward.

179
00:16:35,320 --> 00:16:38,320
There's a law that has changed that in New Hampshire.

180
00:16:38,320 --> 00:16:43,320
And that obligation now rests and sits with this group as the budget committee.

181
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:48,320
So you are now the ones charged with putting forward the default budget for the school district.

182
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:52,320
That is the significant change about where the authority lies.

183
00:16:52,320 --> 00:17:00,320
The statutory formula 40 colon 13 about how the default budget is calculated remains primarily unchanged at this point.

184
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:05,320
I would bring your attention to another bill that's in Senate study right now that's looking to change the formula.

185
00:17:05,320 --> 00:17:06,320
of the default budget.

186
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:08,320
That is not law right now.

187
00:17:08,320 --> 00:17:09,320
It's still in the study committee.

188
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:13,320
But that is something that we will need to work with, Sue, about getting it on an agenda here,

189
00:17:13,320 --> 00:17:17,320
that we are deliberating that and kind of setting that forward for voters as well.

190
00:17:17,320 --> 00:17:22,320
And the third and last piece of legislation I'll bring your attention is some significant law changes

191
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:31,320
that will impact our policy, G-I-C-K, which is essentially the cyber bullying and bullying policy that protects students.

192
00:17:31,320 --> 00:17:38,320
There's some pretty substantial law requirement changes that are going to force the school district to get out and to engage the community a bit more

193
00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:43,320
and to look at some of the retaliatory practices and some of the procedures and timelines that we've set up.

194
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:44,320
So we're beginning that work now.

195
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:50,320
We have a very tight timeline to make those statutory changes, which is January 1st of 2027.

196
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:54,320
So the policy committee on the board level will be working hard at that.

197
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:56,320
That's all I have for business.

198
00:17:56,320 --> 00:17:57,320
Sue?

199
00:17:57,320 --> 00:18:02,320
A couple of things I was thinking about was what would we think about changing?

200
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:03,320
We don't have to.

201
00:18:03,320 --> 00:18:06,320
Changing our time to 6.30 as a beginning.

202
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:07,320
I'd love it.

203
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:08,320
I would hate it.

204
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:09,320
You're okay with that?

205
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:10,320
Or not, Kathryn?

206
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:11,320
No, no way.

207
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:12,320
No.

208
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:14,320
Some of you, I know, have families and need to get home from jobs.

209
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:15,320
Well, it's work too, so.

210
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:16,320
And work.

211
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:17,320
What time to get back?

212
00:18:17,320 --> 00:18:19,320
I rush you to get here.

213
00:18:19,320 --> 00:18:20,320
Seven.

214
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:21,320
Let's keep it at seven.

215
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:28,320
So we'll leave it at seven then, unless there was a consensus that yes, it was okay.

216
00:18:28,320 --> 00:18:33,320
Would we be able to have a workshop on building the default budget?

217
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:38,320
I mean, this is not that we're not familiar with what the default looks like when it's done.

218
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:46,320
But seeing it built and understanding how it's built might be something the committee might be interested in.

219
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:50,320
I don't know what you think about that.

220
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:53,320
Anyone want to speak?

221
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:54,320
Go ahead.

222
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:56,320
How much has it changed since we were around?

223
00:18:56,320 --> 00:19:05,320
Well, if the formula is the same, it's, you know, we have our business people, you know, do the calculations.

224
00:19:05,320 --> 00:19:17,320
I don't think it's going to be huge other than we have the power to say, you know, we want more information or we want to have it clearly explained, which the school board had before.

225
00:19:17,320 --> 00:19:18,320
Right.

226
00:19:18,320 --> 00:19:26,320
So until they pass that Senate bill and get it moved out of a study situation and into law to change the formula.

227
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:27,320
Right.

228
00:19:27,320 --> 00:19:30,320
Still with that same formula.

229
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:40,320
It just, you know, it just deflects the responsibility over here.

230
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:41,320
How much of that formula is discretionary?

231
00:19:41,320 --> 00:19:42,320
Excuse me?

232
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:43,320
Is any of that formula discretionary?

233
